---

## 5. Platform detection — Kunpeng mapping

**`ArmPlatformIdFromMainId`** (`util/platform.cc:142-171`) — the force-map:
```cpp
// util/platform.cc:142
PlatformId ArmPlatformIdFromMainId(uint32_t implementer, uint32_t part_number) {
  if (implementer == 0x41) {
    switch (part_number) {  // ARM IP: Neoverse N1/N2/V2/N3 by part number
      case 0xd0c: return PlatformId::kArmNeoverseN1;
      // ...
    }
  } else if (implementer == 0xc0) {  // Ampere
    if (part_number == 0xac3) return PlatformId::kAmpereOne;
  } else if (implementer == 0x48) {  // Huawei Kunpeng
    return PlatformId::kArmNeoverseN1;  // NOTE: part_number NOT consulted
  }
  return PlatformId::kUndefined;
}
```
Unlike ARM (`0x41`) and Ampere (`0xc0`), the `0x48` (Huawei) branch at `:165-167` does **not** inspect `part_number` — all Kunpeng variants collapse to `kArmNeoverseN1`. There is **no `kArmKunpeng920`** in either enum; Kunpeng snapshots persist under the `ARM_NEOVERSE_N1` (=13) bit.

**MIDR reading** (`util/aarch64/platform.cc:21-49`): `mrs %0, MIDR_EL1` (`:27`) → `implementer = (midr >> 24) & 0xff` (bits [31:24]) → `part_number = (midr >> 4) & 0xfff` (bits [15:4]) → `ArmPlatformIdFromMainId`. Cached in a function-static (`:46`).

**`PlatformId` enum** (`util/platform.h:28-84`): AArch64-relevant values are `kArmNeoverseN1=13`, `kAmpereOne=14`, `kArmNeoverseV2=43`, `kArmNeoverseN3=45`, `kArmNeoverseN2=46`. `PlatformArchitecture` (`util/platform.cc:34-67`) maps all five to `ArchitectureId::kAArch64`.

**Proto mirror** (`proto/snapshot.proto:120-173`): `ARM_NEOVERSE_N1=13` etc., values correspond exactly. Notable: the comment at `:163` says "Ids up to 40 are reserved for proprietary platforms" — a future `kArmKunpeng920` could occupy a reserved slot.

**Cross-check gap**: `common/snapshot_proto.cc:127-128` only `static_assert`s `proto::PlatformId_MAX < 64` (capacity for the `EndState.platforms` bit-vector). Unlike every other enum in that file (which have per-value asserts), PlatformId has **no per-value correspondence enforcement** — a real maintainability gap relevant to a porting paper. Conversion is by `static_cast` in `PlatformsToBitmask`/`BitmaskToPlatforms` (`:326-344`). The unit test (`util/platform_test.cc:159-176`) does **not** cover the `0x48` branch.

---

## Verdict on the paper's central claim

| Claim component | Verdict | Evidence |
|---|---|---|
| "reuse SiliFuzz's snapshot format" | **TRUE** | `proto/snapshot.proto:275-338`, `snap/snap.h:141-198`, `SnapRelocator`, `SnapCorpusHeader` — all reused verbatim |
| "reuse SiliFuzz's runner" | **TRUE** (with AArch64 trampolines added) | `runner/runner.cc`, `RunSnapOutcome` enum (`runner.h:32-43`), `EndSpotToOutcome`, seccomp, nolibc all upstream; only `runner/aarch64/snap_exit.S`, `util/aarch64/start.S`, SVE save/clear are port additions |
| "reuse SiliFuzz's orchestrator" | **TRUE** | `orchestrator/silifuzz_orchestrator.cc` is unmodified upstream, architecture-agnostic |
| "REPLACE the mutation strategy (SiliFuzz byte/bit random → directed)" | **PARTIALLY TRUE, IMPRECISE** | SiliFuzz does NOT do naive byte/bit fuzzing in its documented path. It uses `ProgramBatchMutator` (instruction-aware insert/delete/swap/crossover) whose leaf is a single disassembler-gated bit-flip. The honest contrast is "no directed/semantic targeting of operand values or instruction classes." Only the `--arch`-unset fallback is genuine byte-level. |
| "ADD a gem5-CHAOS fault-injection harness" | **NOT VERIFIABLE** in this repo | No gem5 integration found in the C++ toolchain; this must be an external/added component (the paper's contribution). The SiliFuzz side has a `GEM5_FUZZING_ORIGIN` enum (`proto/snapshot.proto:245`) suggesting an upstream gem5 proxy concept, but no gem5-CHAOS harness exists in this checkout. |

**Recommended claim rewording**: "We reuse SiliFuzz's Snapshot format, Snap relocatable corpus, nolibc/seccomp runner, and orchestrator wholesale. We replace SiliFuzz's instruction-aware-but-undirected mutator (random bit-flip on instruction encodings + random insert/delete/swap/crossover, disassembler-validated) with a directed-mutation-on-random workload generator, and add a gem5-CHAOS fault-injection evaluation harness."

